ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498460 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498460 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498460 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498460 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498460 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cde5acb9-6f89-536b-8c28-e5858a57a739" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186498206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498475 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e6cb29c1-d018-56dc-816a-3dbf5cb4e4f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186497891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186497886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498477 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ef46a245-0a9f-58d0-8e08-5d3259ec7510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498491 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498491 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498491 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498491 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/17/1778ff7ea1ee5b1bcf918992d8b2be700362031cd7d142b00b3dc79470983eb0.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) that water flows through a hose into a tank and the corresponding amount $y$ of water (in gallons) that has entered the tank during that period. What is the unit rate of the water flow?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498491 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ada853a-eb91-5071-a9de-8ca7d83b9586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498491 #edb/ref 1074 "The unit rate in gallons per minute is simply the constant of proportionality for the given proportional relationship.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(4,15).$\n\n![](images/d0/d063a3c2ebf77d1132349b7e48f883632d97720d491a4a5c9158b49c5809258e.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water flow unit rate is $\\frac{15}{4}$ gallons per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498511 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498511 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498511 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498511 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d3/d3de064ab8dbab633656b19cfc340f6ce43f4df8a7e18834019e6301ef67ab00.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $h$ and $x.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f89b123-1c77-5362-ac5f-d71cbdc1fae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498511 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $h$ varies directly with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nh=kx.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $h=6$ and $x=3.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =k\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{6}{3} & =k \\\\ 2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nh=2x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498526 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/df/df0b1950f3093cf368cdd463b1c137df98bae4eb29d7d51f2ce7b34a17c93c77.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship. What is the constant of proportionality?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "476b2500-a5bd-5785-964a-0d0c4c0bd744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498526 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(5,4).$\n\n![](images/46/468695422e5c641a076c8f2889f926a7c633c69e98c96fdefb07b2c19c2acbdc.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{4}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498546 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e1/e19b4c03ff6ee1555c80aa76f79fadd2ae768d8cb5ca68631a2027d58e14829d.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above represent proportional relationships?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6bdc2d08-aee3-51a7-842c-23db97075a50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498546 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the graph of a proportional relationship is always a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nOf the given graphs, only graph II fits this description.\n\n- Graphs I and III are not straight lines.\n\n- Graph IV does not pass through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498547 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498547 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498547 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498547 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/47/472c0aebabed979b97d9d68648785773f0715da9e8cf88c1f1ee912a27d6cb77.png)\n\nThe graph above shows that the distance $d$ an electric train travels is directly proportional to the number of hours $t$ since its journey started. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $d$ and $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498547 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "700640db-e0fe-571f-9cca-6b3afdd864a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498547 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $d$ varies directly with $t,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nd=kt.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $t=4$ and $d=840.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}840 & =k\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{840}{4} & =k \\\\ 210 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nd=210t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498595 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186498434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498595 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498595 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498595 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/35/355a42a39049738de0a24fa57dda5552e06900b253afccf012e0024a38191e09.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above represent proportional relationships?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed2c06d4-147d-5982-8675-5c72245a447f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498595 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the graph of a proportional relationship is always a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nOf the given graphs, none of the graphs fits this description.\n\n- Graphs I, II, and III  are not straight lines.\n\n- Graph  IV does not pass through the origin $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
